      "title": "Google chat log deletion policy",
      "summary": "In February, the DOJ accused Google of systematically destroying chats via a history-off option deleting messages every 24 hours; Pichai claimed he took action against the history-off default in February to comply with the DOJ's litigation hold, though Bellshaw cited a 2021 message exchange where Pichai discussed turning off history for a personnel matter unrelated to the hold."
    },
    {
      "title": "Internet Explorer default comparison",
      "summary": "In 2005, Microsoft made Internet Explorer the default search engine, prompting Google's legal chief David Drummond to send an angry letter accusing this of anticompetitive behavior; Bellshaw used Drummond's letter to highlight Google's hypocrisy regarding defaults, noting that changes to defaults worsen problems and most users do not change them, arguments also used by DuckDuckGo, Brave, and Bing against Google."
    },
    {
      "title": "Antitrust case implications and data",
      "summary": "The case is expected to continue for several weeks, potentially resulting in a Microsoft-like outcome where Google must change behavior, share APIs, and be banned from exclusive deals; Google might have to turn over collected data to other search engines, with the DOJ noting Google gets 16 times more data than Bing daily; the outcome could impact FTC cases against Amazon and Apple, and an ongoing FTC case against Facebook to sell Instagram and WhatsApp."
